The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020) and 2585 (2021), and its presidential statements of 3 August 2011 (S/PRST/2011/16), 21 March 2012 (S/PRST/2012/6), 5 April 2012 (S/PRST/2012/10), 2 October 2013 (S/PRST/2013/15), 24 April 2015 (S/PRST/2015/10), 17 August 2015 (S/PRST/2015/15), and 8 October 2019 (S/PRST/2019/12),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of Syria and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,
Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Syrian authorities to protect the Syrian population and to facilitate the delivery of humanitarian assistance throughout the territory of Syria, and stressing that all parties must respect international humanitarian law,
Recalling that humanitarian assistance should be provided in accordance with the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance, including humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,
Stressing the need for full, safe and unhindered humanitarian access, without delay, to all parts of Syria, and emphasizing the need to expand cross-line deliveries from within Syria in order to improve the transparency, effectiveness and sustainability of humanitarian operations,
Recalling paragraph 2 of resolution 2585 (2021) and the request for a substantive report by the Secretary-General with particular focus on transparency in operations and progress on cross-line access, and recalling the temporary and exceptional nature of the cross-border mechanism,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Syria, including the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, the economic crisis, food insecurity, drought and displacement, and underlining the need to scale up early recovery projects, including in the areas of water, sanitation, health, education and shelter,
Stressing the need for transparency, accountability and the efficient use of humanitarian assistance, and welcoming the efforts of the United Nations, its specialized agencies, the International Committee of the Red Cross and other humanitarian partners,
Noting the importance of ensuring that humanitarian assistance and early recovery activities are not impeded by sanctions or counter-terrorism measures, and recalling the relevant provisions of international law,
Determining that the devastating humanitarian situation in Syria continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,
1. Demands the full and immediate implementation of all provisions of all relevant Security Council resolutions, including resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020) and 2585 (2021);
2. Decides to extend the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of resolution 2165 (2014) for a period of six months, that is, until 10 January 2023, only for the border crossing at Bab al-Hawa, subject to the issuance by the Secretary-General of the substantive report requested in resolution 2585 (2021), with particular focus on transparency in operations and progress on cross-line access in meeting humanitarian needs;
3. Calls upon all parties to ensure full, safe, unhindered and sustained humanitarian access to all parts of Syria, including through the expansion of cross-line deliveries from Damascus across conflict lines, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance, and encourages the United Nations and its humanitarian partners to strengthen cross-line operations and early recovery projects in coordination with relevant parties;
4. Requests the Secretary-General to ensure that the cross-border mechanism is used in full transparency and in strict compliance with the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance, and to prioritize, where feasible, cross-line deliveries in meeting humanitarian needs;
5. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to brief the Council monthly and to provide a substantive report, at least every 60 days, on the implementation of this resolution and resolution 2585 (2021), including detailed information on humanitarian assistance delivered through the Bab al-Hawa crossing, the distribution mechanism, beneficiaries, operating partners, locations, volume and nature of items delivered, and on progress achieved in expanding cross-line deliveries and early recovery projects;
6. Calls upon Member States to respond with practical steps to address the urgent needs of the Syrian people, including through support for early recovery projects in water, sanitation, health, education and shelter, and to ensure that sanctions and counter-terrorism measures do not impede humanitarian assistance and early recovery activities;
7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

